## 2. Sub-question answers

### Q: Does tokio automatically reap the process anyway?

**On Unix: yes, best-effort, via a global orphan queue.**
**On Windows: there is nothing to reap, and tokio does no adoption whatsoever.**

The public `Child` type has **no `Drop` impl** in `src/process/mod.rs` (grep for `impl Drop` returns only `ChildDropGuard`, whose only job is the optional `kill_on_drop` kill). The interesting drops live on the platform-internal `imp::Child`:

```rust
// src/process/unix/reap.rs:117-130
impl&lt;W, Q, S&gt; Drop for Reaper&lt;W, Q, S&gt;
where
    W: Wait,
    Q: OrphanQueue&lt;W&gt;,
{
    fn drop(&amp;mut self) {
        if let Ok(Some(_)) = self.inner_mut().try_wait() {
            return;                       // already exited -&gt; std reaped it via waitpid
        }

        let orphan = self.inner.take().unwrap();
        self.orphan_queue.push_orphan(orphan);   // &lt;-- ADOPTION
    }
}
```

```rust
// src/process/unix/pidfd_reaper.rs:198-211  (Linux + feature &quot;rt&quot;, the common path)
impl&lt;W, Q&gt; Drop for PidfdReaper&lt;W, Q&gt;
where
    W: Wait + Unpin,
    Q: OrphanQueue&lt;W&gt; + Unpin,
{
    fn drop(&amp;mut self) {
        let mut orphan = self.inner.take().expect(&quot;inner has gone away&quot;).inner;
        if let Ok(Some(_)) = orphan.try_wait() {
            return;
        }

        self.orphan_queue.push_orphan(orphan);   // &lt;-- ADOPTION
    }
}
```

Note the eager `try_wait()` first: if the process already exited, the drop is a no-op (dropping the `std::process::Child` after a successful `try_wait` is just handle cleanup). Otherwise the `std::process::Child` is **moved into a global queue** — it is *not* dropped.

### Q: Is there a zombie-process problem on Unix?

Yes, but it is bounded and self-healing, and only on the order of milliseconds. The queue is drained by `try_wait()` in a loop, which on Unix **is** `waitpid(WNOHANG)` and therefore genuinely reaps:

```rust
// src/process/unix/orphan.rs:113-130
fn drain_orphan_queue&lt;T&gt;(mut queue: MutexGuard&lt;&#39;_, Vec&lt;T&gt;&gt;)
where
    T: Wait,
{
    for i in (0..queue.len()).rev() {
        match queue[i].try_wait() {
            Ok(None) =&gt; {}                       // still running -&gt; keep in queue
            Ok(Some(_)) | Err(_) =&gt; {
                // The stdlib handles interruption errors (EINTR) when polling a child process.
                // All other errors represent invalid inputs or pids that have already been
                // reaped, so we can drop the orphan in case an error is raised.
                queue.swap_remove(i);            // reaped or un-reapable -&gt; remove
            }
        }
    }
    drop(queue);
}
```

Tokio&#39;s own docs state the guarantee level plainly:

```
// src/process/mod.rs:211-223
//! ## Unix Processes
//!
//! On Unix platforms processes must be &quot;reaped&quot; by their parent process after
//! they have exited in order to release all OS resources. ...
//!
//! The tokio runtime will, on a best-effort basis, attempt to reap and clean up
//! any process which it has spawned. No additional guarantees are made with regard to
//! how quickly or how often this procedure will take place.
//!
//! It is recommended to avoid dropping a [`Child`] process handle before it has been
//! fully `await`ed if stricter cleanup guarantees are required.
```

### Q: Is there an unreaped-HANDLE / resource-leak problem on Windows?

**No.** The Windows `imp::Child` has **no `Drop` impl whatsoever** — and it does not need one:

```rust
// src/process/windows.rs:44-47
pub(crate) struct Child {
    child: StdChild,          // std::process::Child
    waiting: Option&lt;Waiting&gt;,
}
```

`windows.rs` contains exactly one `Drop` impl — `impl Drop for Waiting` at line 152 — and that exists only to `UnregisterWaitEx` the `RegisterWaitForSingleObject` registration, not to reap anything:

```rust
// src/process/windows.rs:152-162
impl Drop for Waiting {
    fn drop(&amp;mut self) {
        unsafe {
            let rc = UnregisterWaitEx(self.wait_object, INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE);
            if rc == 0 {
                panic!(&quot;failed to unregister: {}&quot;, io::Error::last_os_error());
            }
            drop(Box::from_raw(self.tx));
        }
    }
}
```

So on Windows, dropping the `Child` cascades:

```
tokio::Child
  └─ FusedChild::Child(ChildDropGuard&lt;imp::Child&gt;)   // drop: kill only if kill_on_drop
       └─ imp::Child { child: StdChild, waiting: None }
            └─ std::process::Child
                 └─ handle: Handle   →  pub struct Handle(OwnedHandle)
                      └─ OwnedHandle →  impl Drop { CloseHandle(self.handle) }
```

The terminal `Drop` is in std:

```rust
// library/std/src/os/windows/io/handle.rs:382-390
impl Drop for OwnedHandle {
    #[inline]
    fn drop(&amp;mut self) {
        unsafe {
            let _ = sys::c::CloseHandle(self.handle);
        }
    }
}
```

`CloseHandle` on a process handle is the correct and complete release operation on Windows. There is no zombie concept.

