<div class="entry entry-assistant" id="entry-225"><div class="label">Assistant</div><p>Yes and no — that's the crux, and the distinction matters for the fix.</p>
<p><strong>The tool is different. The role is the same.</strong></p>
<p>Two separate binaries are involved:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The linker</strong> — <code>/usr/bin/lld-link</code>, standalone LLVM LLD 22.1.8 from your distro. This is what prints LNK4099. It is <em>not</em> part of the Rust toolchain at all; it's the system <code>lld</code> package, the same one that provides <code>ld.lld</code>.</li>
<li><strong>The lint</strong> — <code>linker_messages</code>, a rustc lint. It runs inside rustc, captures whatever the linker wrote to stderr, and re-emits it as a Rust <code>warning:</code> with a <code>linker stderr:</code> banner.</li>
</ul>
<p>That separation is the whole reason the output looks like a Rust problem. lld-link did the work and said one thing; rustc re-presented it.</p>
<p><strong>But your build isn't using the system lld-link.</strong> I checked, and this is worth being precise about. Your justfile sets:</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">export CARGO_TARGET_X86_64_PC_WINDOWS_MSVC_LINKER=lld-link
</code></pre>
<p>That resolves to the system <code>/usr/bin/lld-link</code>. Separately, the Rust toolchain ships its own <code>rust-lld</code> — I found it in both your stable and nightly toolchains. Rust uses <code>rust-lld</code> by default for many targets and only falls back to a system linker when told to, which is what your env var does here.</p>
<p>Either way, <strong>it's the same LLD codebase</strong> — <code>rust-lld</code> is just LLD built as a rustc-invoked wrapper. The LNK4099 text and behavior are identical, which is why the message format is Microsoft-compatible.</p>
<h2>Why this matters for the fix</h2>
<p>The two levers act at different layers, and that's the real tradeoff I was circling earlier:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Lever</th><th>Layer</th><th>Hides</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>-A linker_messages</code></td><td>rustc lint</td><td><strong>all</strong> linker stderr promoted to warnings</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>-Clink-arg=/ignore:4099</code></td><td>lld-link itself</td><td><strong>only</strong> LNK4099</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><code>-A</code> is the blunt instrument: it silences the entire category, including warnings you might actually want. <code>/ignore:4099</code> tells lld-link to drop that one code at the source, so it never reaches the lint, and a real diagnostic like LNK4217 still surfaces.</p>
<p>Given you've now made clear you won't be debugging this build but <em>will</em> be reading these logs for genuine problems, <code>/ignore:4099</code> expresses your actual intent better. I've been recommending <code>-A</code> largely because it's the one I proved, and I'd rather not hand you a claim I haven't run.</p>
<p>The verification is one build. No edits to your files. Say go and I'll confirm before it goes in the plan.</p>
