<h2>So, natively, rustc uses the system linker</h2>
<p>Not a bundled one. <code>cc</code> is your system compiler driver (gcc or clang), and the two flags matter:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>-B…/gcc-ld</code> — points the driver at the <code>gcc-ld</code> wrapper directory Rust ships in the toolchain.</li>
<li><code>-fuse-ld=lld</code> — tells that wrapper to use LLD as the backend.</li>
</ul>
<p>The net effect: <strong>system <code>cc</code> as the driver, LLD as the actual linker.</strong> Rust ships no complete native linker; it borrows your system toolchain and steers it toward LLD. That's why removing <code>lld</code> from your system would break Rust builds on Linux even though <code>ld.lld</code> &quot;belongs to Rust.&quot;</p>
<p>And note the log line that resolves the naming confusion from earlier — the same <code>linker_messages</code> plumbing is active on this native build too:</p>
<pre><code>INFO rustc_codegen_ssa::back::link reporting linker output: flavor=Gnu(Yes, Yes)
INFO … linker stderr:
</code></pre>
<p>Rust captures linker stderr on <em>every</em> platform and runs the lint. It just never fires on Linux because <code>ld.lld</code> doesn't emit LNK-numbered messages — GNU-style and LLD's ELF diagnostics are terse, and nothing references missing PDBs. On MSVC targets it fires constantly because the message format is verbose and the CRT has dangling references.</p>
