<div class="entry entry-assistant" id="entry-277"><div class="label">Assistant</div><p>Microsoft's debug-info format for Windows — the PDB counterpart to DWARF on Linux.</p>
<p>Debug builds emit <code>.pdb</code> files (a separate, compact binary format) holding types, variable names, line tables. Objects reference them through a debug directory entry, and debuggers read the two together to map machine code back to source. LLVM supports it natively, so rustc's Windows builds use it too.</p>
</div>
<div class="entry entry-user" id="entry-278"><div class="label">You</div><p>if the path is required but it will be different on every build then how does the symbol server match it up</p>
</div>
<div class="entry entry-assistant" id="entry-279"><div class="label">Assistant</div><p>It doesn't use the path at all.</p>
<p>The GUID is a content hash — generated at build time from the compiler version, flags, sources, and toolchain. Same inputs, same GUID. The age increments on every rebuild of that same source tree. So a symbol server indexes by GUID+age, and the lookup is <code>Given a GUID, hand me the PDB</code>. The path is written for the file-open fallback and is simply wrong in the cross case — irrelevant when the server answers.</p>
</div>
<div class="entry entry-user" id="entry-280"><div class="label">You</div><p>if nothing uses the path then why dont they strip it</p>
</div>
<div class="entry entry-assistant" id="entry-281"><div class="label">Assistant</div><p>Because the format requires it, and the format is Microsoft's.</p>
<p><code>IMAGE_DEBUG_TYPE_CODEVIEW</code> mandates the path field — it's a fixed-layout record, not optional metadata. You can't null it out without breaking every tool that reads the entry, including the ones that don't use the GUID. Same reason you can't remove a field from a struct: the layout is the contract.</p>
<p>It's a vestigial field kept for compatibility, not a live mechanism. It just happens to be the only one that works when no symbol server is available — which is your case.</p>
</div>
